  <h1>Lesson 4</h1>
  <p>The Circular Flow of Income</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

  <div class="lesson-info">
    <h3>Specification Reference</h3>
    <p>
      OxfordAQA AS Economics Unit 2 — 3.2.2.1 The circular flow of income
    </p>

    <h3>Learning Objectives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Understand what national income measures</li>
      <li>Distinguish between nominal income and real income</li>
      <li>Explain why real national income is used as an indicator of economic performance</li>
      <li>Understand the circular flow of income model</li>
      <li>Explain the equation income = output = expenditure</li>
      <li>Understand equilibrium income and full employment income</li>
      <li>Distinguish between injections and withdrawals</li>
      <li>Analyse how changes in injections and withdrawals affect national income</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Mind Map Overview</h2>

    <div class="mindmap-diagram">
      <div class="mindmap-root">
        The circular flow of income explains how income, output and expenditure move through the macroeconomy
      </div>

      <div class="mindmap-level-1">

        <div class="mindmap-branch">
          <div class="branch-title measure">Measuring National Income</div>

          <div class="sub-branch measure-box">
            <h4>National income</h4>
            <p>Total income earned by factors of production in an economy.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch measure-box">
            <h4>Nominal income</h4>
            <p>Measured in current money terms.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch measure-box">
            <h4>Real income</h4>
            <p>Adjusted for inflation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mindmap-branch">
          <div class="branch-title model">Circular Flow Model</div>

          <div class="sub-branch model-box">
            <h4>Households</h4>
            <p>Provide factors of production and receive income.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch model-box">
            <h4>Firms</h4>
            <p>Produce goods and services and pay factor incomes.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch model-box">
            <h4>Core equation</h4>
            <p>Income = Output = Expenditure</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mindmap-branch">
          <div class="branch-title leakage">Injections and Withdrawals</div>

          <div class="sub-branch leakage-box">
            <h4>Injections</h4>
            <p>Investment, government spending and exports.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch leakage-box">
            <h4>Withdrawals</h4>
            <p>Saving, taxation and imports.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch leakage-box">
            <h4>Impact</h4>
            <p>More injections increase income; more withdrawals reduce income.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mindmap-branch">
          <div class="branch-title equilibrium">Equilibrium Income</div>

          <div class="sub-branch equilibrium-box">
            <h4>Equilibrium income</h4>
            <p>Occurs when injections equal withdrawals.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch equilibrium-box">
            <h4>Full employment income</h4>
            <p>Income level when resources are fully employed.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-branch equilibrium-box">
            <h4>Output gap</h4>
            <p>Actual income may be below or above full employment income.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>2. What Does National Income Measure?</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      National income measures the total income earned by factors of production in an economy over a period of time.
    </div>

    <p>
      National income is closely linked to the total value of output produced in the economy.
      When firms produce goods and services, they pay incomes to households in the form of wages, rent, interest and profit.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      National income = Total income earned from producing goods and services
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <strong>Example:</strong><br>
      If firms produce more output, they usually need more labour and other resources. This increases the income paid to households.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Nominal Income and Real Income</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <h3>3.1 Nominal Income</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Nominal income is income measured in current money terms, without adjusting for inflation.
    </div>

    <p>
      Nominal income may rise simply because prices have increased.
    </p>

    <h3>3.2 Real Income</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Real income is income adjusted for inflation.
    </div>

    <p>
      Real income is more useful for measuring economic performance because it shows whether people can actually buy more goods and services.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      Real income = Nominal income / Price index × 100
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <strong>Example:</strong><br>
      If nominal national income rises by 8% but prices rise by 5%, real national income rises by approximately 3%.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>4. Real National Income as an Indicator of Economic Performance</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <p>
      Real national income is an important indicator because it measures the value of output after removing the effects of inflation.
    </p>

    <p>
      If real national income increases, it suggests that the economy is producing more goods and services.
    </p>

    <div class="evaluation">
      However, real national income does not show income distribution, environmental costs, unpaid work or changes in quality of life.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>5. The Circular Flow of Income Model</h2>

  <div class="flow-diagram">
    <div class="flow-grid">
      <div class="flow-card households">Households</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">Spending on goods and services →</div>

      <div class="flow-arrow">← Income: wages, rent, interest, profit</div>
      <div class="flow-card firms">Firms</div>
    </div>

    <p class="flow-note">
      Households provide factors of production to firms. Firms produce goods and services and pay income to households.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="box">
    <p>
      In the simplest circular flow model, there are two main groups:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Households</strong> own factors of production and receive income.</li>
      <li><strong>Firms</strong> use factors of production to produce goods and services.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Households spend their income on goods and services produced by firms.
      Firms then use this revenue to pay households for the factors of production.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>6. Income = Output = Expenditure</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Key idea:</strong><br>
      The value of national income, national output and national expenditure should be equal.
    </div>

    <div class="formula">
      Income = Output = Expenditure
    </div>

    <p>
      This is because one person’s spending becomes another person’s income.
      When firms sell output, they receive expenditure from households.
      This revenue is then paid out as income to workers and owners of factors of production.
    </p>

    <div class="example">
      <strong>Example:</strong><br>
      A student spends $10 on lunch. This $10 is expenditure for the student, revenue for the firm and income for workers or business owners.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>7. Equilibrium Income and Full Employment Income</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <h3>7.1 Equilibrium Income</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Equilibrium income is the level of national income where planned injections equal planned withdrawals.
    </div>

    <div class="formula">
      Injections = Withdrawals
    </div>

    <p>
      At equilibrium, there is no tendency for national income to change.
    </p>

    <h3>7.2 Full Employment Income</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Full employment income is the level of national income where the economy is using its resources fully.
    </div>

    <p>
      Equilibrium income does not always equal full employment income.
      An economy can be in equilibrium while still having unemployment.
    </p>

    <div class="evaluation">
      If equilibrium income is below full employment income, there is likely to be a negative output gap and cyclical unemployment.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>8. Injections and Withdrawals</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <h3>8.1 Injections</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Injections are additions of spending into the circular flow of income.
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Investment:</strong> spending by firms on capital goods</li>
      <li><strong>Government spending:</strong> spending by the government on goods and services</li>
      <li><strong>Exports:</strong> spending by foreign consumers and firms on domestic goods and services</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="formula">
      Injections = I + G + X
    </div>

    <h3>8.2 Withdrawals</h3>

    <div class="definition">
      <strong>Definition:</strong><br>
      Withdrawals are leakages of income from the circular flow.
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Saving:</strong> income not spent by households</li>
      <li><strong>Taxation:</strong> income taken by the government</li>
      <li><strong>Imports:</strong> spending on foreign goods and services</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="formula">
      Withdrawals = S + T + M
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>9. Effects of Changes in Injections and Withdrawals</h2>

  <div class="box">
    <h3>When injections increase</h3>

    <div class="formula">
      I / G / X ↑ → AD ↑ → National income ↑ → Employment ↑
    </div>

    <p>
      An increase in injections adds extra spending to the circular flow.
      This increases aggregate demand and can increase national income.
    </p>

    <h3>When withdrawals increase</h3>

    <div class="formula">
      S / T / M ↑ → Spending in circular flow ↓ → National income ↓
    </div>

    <p>
      An increase in withdrawals removes spending from the circular flow.
      This can reduce aggregate demand and lower national income.
    </p>

    <div class="evaluation">
      The final effect depends on the size of the change, the multiplier, spare capacity and business confidence.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>10. Clicker Example: Equilibrium Income</h2>

  <div class="clicker-box">
    <h3>Example</h3>

    <p>
      In an economy:<br>
      Investment = $40bn<br>
      Government spending = $60bn<br>
      Exports = $30bn<br>
      Saving = $50bn<br>
      Taxation = $55bn<br>
      Imports = $25bn
    </p>

    <button class="clicker-btn" onclick="showNextStep('equilibrium')">Show next step</button>
    <button class="reset-btn" onclick="resetSteps('equilibrium')">Reset</button>

    <div class="step" data-step-group="equilibrium">
      <strong>Step 1: Calculate injections</strong><br>
      I + G + X = 40 + 60 + 30 = $130bn
    </div>

    <div class="step" data-step-group="equilibrium">
      <strong>Step 2: Calculate withdrawals</strong><br>
      S + T + M = 50 + 55 + 25 = $130bn
    </div>

    <div class="step" data-step-group="equilibrium">
      <strong>Step 3: Compare injections and withdrawals</strong><br>
      Injections = Withdrawals
    </div>

    <div class="step" data-step-group="equilibrium">
      <strong>Step 4: Interpret</strong><br>
      The economy is in equilibrium because planned injections equal planned withdrawals.
    </div>
  </div>

